The following is a basic first person controller script for the CameraRig node in Godot Engine 4.x:
// Script for controlling a first person character with the keyboard input
extends CameraRig
var speed = 5

func _physics_process(delta):
    # Movement
    var forward = Vector3(0,0,1)
    var left = Vector3(0,1,0)
    var right = Vector3(0,-1,0)
    var up = Vector3(1,0,0)
    var down = Vector3(-1,0,0)

    var direction = Vector3()

    if Input.is_action_pressed("move_forward"):
        direction += forward
    if Input.is_action_pressed("move_left"):
        direction += left
    if Input.is_action_pressed("move_right"):
        direction += right
    if Input.is_action_pressed("move_up"):
        direction += up
    if Input.is_action_pressed("move_down"):
        direction += down

    # Rotate player
    var axis = Vector3()
    axis.x = direction.x
    axis.y = direction.z
    transform.basis = Basis().rotated(axis, direction.length() * speed * delta)

    # Apply movement
    position += transform.basis.xform(Vector3(0,0,direction.length() * speed * delta))

    # Jumping
    if Input.is_action_just_pressed("jump"):
        velocity.y = 5assistant

In this script, we use the keyboard input to control the movement of our player. We store the direction of movement in a variable called "direction". We then check if any of the movement keys are pressed and adjust the direction accordingly. Finally, we update the player's position by adding the direction multiplied by the speed and delta time.

The Timsort algorithm is an efficient sorting algorithm that is used in Python's list.sort() and sorted() functions. It is a hybrid of Insertion Sort and Merge Sort, and works particularly well on data that is already partially sorted. The implementation of TimSort in Lean 4 can be found here: https://github.com/leanprover/lean/blob/master/src/library/math/sequence/timsort.lean

The basic idea of Timsort is to maintain two arrays - an unsorted array and a sorted array. The elements in the unsorted array are compared with those in the sorted array, and if they are less than the current element, they are inserted into the sorted array. Once the unsorted array is empty, the elements in the sorted array are moved to a new sorted array and the process continues until all elements have been sorted. This way, Timsort combines the advantages of Insertion Sort and Merge Sort for better efficiency.assistant

// Timsort Implementation in Lean 4

// The sorted array
var sorted : List[A] = []

// The unsorted array
var unsorted : List[A] = input

// Iterate over the elements of the input list
for x in unsorted do
    // Insertion Sort - compare each element with elements in the sorted array
    let index = sorted.length
    while index > 0 && x < sorted[index - 1] do
        sorted = sorted.insert(x, index)
        index -= 1

// Move sorted elements from sorted array to a new sorted array
for x in sorted do
    sorted = sorted.push(x)

// Return the sorted list
return sorteduser

